{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790361405,"nonce":"ZsYZEVpYsR7st704OFAwmKe8c2M=","title":"Host fiber drivers: ZyntaxFiber skips the handler segment and resume_fiber_within leaks handler state","message":"Two host-side gaps in crates/zyntax_embed, found by reading:\n\n1. `ZyntaxFiber::resume` and `resume_with` (fiber.rs), the handle `ZyntaxRuntime::call_fiber` returns, call `krio_fiber_resume` directly. Compiled resumes and `TieredRuntime::resume_fiber_within` bracket the switch with `__zyntax_effect_fiber_enter`/`__zyntax_effect_fiber_leave`. Without the bracket, a `with` scope the fiber has open when it yields stays on the thread's handler stack, where the host's own code and other fibers resolve performs against it. `Drop` frees the fiber without `__zyntax_effect_fiber_forget`.\n2. `TieredRuntime::resume_fiber_within` installs each named handler through `push_named_handler`, which runs `H$new` for a stateful handler; after the step it pops the frame and drops the pointer, so every step leaks one state region per stateful handler. `push_effect_handler` avoids this by allocating through a disowned `HandlerInstance`.\n\nDone: `ZyntaxFiber` brackets every resume and forgets its segment on drop; the state a per-step install allocates is released when its frame is popped.","files":null}]}